// ui::layout: the solver (plan section 2.4, "Solve"). `end_frame` calls `solve`, `solve` once more
// when a scroll region gained or lost a bar, and `position` alone when something only moved.
//
// How an element is sized along an axis (`ui::Size`):
//
//   fix    exactly its value.
//   FIT    its content plus its padding, held between `min` and `max`. A text's content is its
//          line (for wrapped text its longest paragraph); a box's is its flow children: their
//          sizes and the gaps between them added up along its axis, the largest of them across it.
//   GROW   starts as FIT. Along its parent's axis it then shares the parent's spare room with the
//          other grow children; when the parent is short it gives room up instead. Across the
//          parent's axis it fills the parent's inner size. Always held between `min` and `max`.
//   take   that fraction of the parent's inner size (less the gaps along the parent's axis), held
//          between `min` and `max`.
//
// The steps, in the order of the layout this one is modelled on (ZGF's `LayoutTree.Solve`):
//
//   1. Fit widths, last element to first, so children before parents.
//   2. Share widths, first element to last, so a parent's width is final before its children's:
//      `take` is resolved, a grow child across the axis fills, and along the axis spare room goes
//      to the grow children, or is taken back from them.
//   3. Text, at the widths now known: wrapped text is broken into lines, a single line marked
//      `Overflow::ellipsis` that does not fit is cut short and gets its dots.
//   4. Fit heights, then share heights, the same way. A wrapped text is as high as its lines.
//   5. Positions, first element to last: a root at its anchor on the surface; flow children one
//      after another along the parent's axis from its padding edge, `gap` apart, moved by
//      `spare * align` on each axis (spare is never negative, so children that do not fit start
//      at the start edge and run past the far one) and by the parent's scroll offset; placed
//      children at parent.xy + parent.wh * anchor + offset.
//
// A paint takes no part: it is drawn in its parent's coordinates. A placed child and a root add
// nothing to a parent's size and take none of its room.
//
// Where the plan says nothing, the rule is ZGF's unless a line here says otherwise:
//
//  - Sharing is of the spare room, not of the parent: every grow child gets the same amount on
//    top of what it was fitted to, so three empty grow children end up equal, and one that holds
//    content (or has a `min`) ends up that much larger than the others.
//  - Nothing is rounded: a share is a fraction, and emit puts the edges on the device grid.
//  - A grow child stops at its `max`, and what it could not take is shared again among the
//    rest; a shrinking one stops at its `min`. Each of these rounds gives every child that can
//    still change the same amount, and there are at most ROUNDS of them, so a parent whose
//    grow children stop at more than ROUNDS different sizes may leave a little room unshared.
//    Room smaller than SLACK is not shared at all.
//  - When grow children are all at their `min` and the parent is still short, nothing else
//    shrinks: the children overflow from the start edge. FIT, fix and take never give room up.
//  - A parent is fitted before any room is shared, so a FIT parent has no spare room: its grow
//    children along its axis keep their fitted sizes, and those across it fill it, which makes
//    them as large as the largest of their siblings.
//  - A `take` child counts as its `min` (0 unless set) when its parent is fitted, and then takes
//    its fraction of what the parent came to; in a FIT parent that makes the children overflow.
//    (ZGF counts it as 0 even when it has a `min`; with the default `min` the two are the same.)
//  - `min` wins when it is larger than `max`.
//  - A placed child's `take` is a fraction of its parent's whole size, padding included, and a
//    placed GROW child fills that size on both axes. A root stands in the surface the same way.
//  - Text. Wrapped text whose width is FIT is sized as GROW, so it fills a column, and in a row
//    it takes the spare room or gives room up and wraps. A single line marked
//    `Overflow::ellipsis` whose width is FIT is sized as GROW with its own width as `max`: it
//    never grows past its text and gives room up when its parent is short. Any other width (fix,
//    take, GROW, and FIT for text that neither wraps nor has dots) is taken as declared. A line
//    is cut for its dots only when it is more than FITS wider than its room. Text that does not
//    wrap is one line whatever it holds.
//  - A text that finds the line table full is not laid out, or not past the lines that fitted,
//    and is counted once a frame however often the frame is solved.
//
// The solver cannot fail. It walks the flat array and never recurses; apart from the rounds of
// sharing, each bounded by ROUNDS passes over one parent's children, its work is linear in the
// elements. Every size it stores is a number from 0 to LIMIT whatever it was given: a size that
// is not a number is 0, and nothing is divided by zero. Positions are sums of those sizes and of
// the padding, gap, anchor, offset and scroll the program gave, as they were given.
//
// What a solve leaves in an element: `x`, `y`, `w`, `h`; for a box, `content_w` and `content_h`,
// the room its flow children take without its padding (their sizes and gaps along its axis, the
// largest of them across it) once every size is final, which is what a scroll region measures
// its range by; for a text, `first_line`, `line_count` and `dots`, with its lines in
// `ui.layout.lines` (byte ranges of the element's own text, and each line's width), and
// `content_h`, the height of those lines. A text's `content_w` stays the width of its one line.
// Emit reads `x`, `y`, `w`, `h` and a text's lines, and nothing else.
namespace ui {
    namespace layout {
        // No size is larger than this.
        const LIMIT: f32 = 1000000000.0
        // Spare or missing room smaller than this is left as it is.
        const SLACK: f32 = 0.01
        // How many times room is shared again among the children that can still take or give it.
        const ROUNDS: u32 = 32
        // A single line this much wider than its room is still drawn whole.
        const FITS: f32 = 0.5

        // The lines of every text element of the frame, in element order. `reported` is how many
        // texts this frame has counted as left without their lines.
        struct State { lines: []mut text::Line, line_count: u32, reported: u32 }

        fn make_state(S) { realloc: alloc::Fn(S), mut heap: S, cap: Capacity } -> !State {
            let lines = try alloc::make(text::Line, S){ realloc, &heap, count = @as(usize, cap.lines) }
            return State{ lines, line_count = 0, reported = 0 }
        }

        fn reset_state { mut ui: Ui } {
            ui.layout.line_count = 0
            ui.layout.reported = 0
        }

        // Solves the whole frame. It may run again on the same tree: every step starts from what
        // was recorded, not from what the last solve left.
        fn solve { mut ui: Ui } {
            fit_sizes{ &ui, wide = true }
            share_sizes{ &ui, wide = true }
            lay_text{ &ui }
            fit_sizes{ &ui, wide = false }
            share_sizes{ &ui, wide = false }
            position{ &ui }
        }

        // Whether a child takes room in its parent's flow.
        fn is_flow { e: tree::Element } -> bool {
            return e.kind != tree::Kind::paint and not e.placed and not e.root
        }

        // 0 for start, 0.5 for center, 1 for end.
        fn get_factor { align: Align } -> f32 {
            match align {
                start => { return 0.0 }
                center => { return 0.5 }
                end => { return 1.0 }
            }
        }

        // `v` as a size: 0 when it is negative or not a number, and never more than LIMIT.
        fn hold_size { v: f32 } -> f32 {
            if not (v > 0.0) { return 0.0 }
            return if v > LIMIT { LIMIT } else { v }
        }

        // The padding of `e` along one axis, both sides together.
        fn sum_padding { e: tree::Element, wide: bool } -> f32 {
            return hold_size{ v = if wide { e.padding.left + e.padding.right } else { e.padding.top + e.padding.bottom } }
        }

        // How `e` is sized along one axis. It is what was declared, but for the width of a text
        // that was left FIT (the rules are at the top of the file): wrapped text grows, and a
        // single line with dots may give room up but never grows past its own width.
        fn get_size { e: tree::Element, wide: bool } -> Size {
            let size = if wide { e.width } else { e.height }
            if not wide or e.kind != tree::Kind::text or size.kind != SizeKind::fit { return size }
            if e.wrap { return Size{ kind = SizeKind::grow, value = size.value, min = size.min, max = size.max } }
            if e.overflow == Overflow::ellipsis {
                let own = e.content_w + sum_padding{ e, wide }
                return Size{ kind = SizeKind::grow, value = size.value, min = size.min, max = pick_max_f32{ a = size.min, b = pick_min_f32{ a = size.max, b = own } } }
            }
            return size
        }

        // The size an element starts with, before its parent shares out any room: `content` is
        // its content with its padding.
        fn fit_size { size: Size, content: f32 } -> f32 {
            match size.kind {
                fixed => { return hold_size{ v = size.value } }
                part => { return hold_size{ v = clamp_f32{ v = 0.0, lo = size.min, hi = size.max } } }
                else => { return hold_size{ v = clamp_f32{ v = content, lo = size.min, hi = size.max } } }
            }
        }

        // The size of an element once the room it stands in is known: a `take` its fraction of
        // `basis`, a GROW that is `filling` all of `basis`, anything else the size it was fitted to.
        fn take_room { size: Size, fitted: f32, basis: f32, filling: bool } -> f32 {
            match size.kind {
                part => { return hold_size{ v = clamp_f32{ v = basis * size.value, lo = size.min, hi = size.max } } }
                grow => {
                    if not filling { return fitted }
                    return hold_size{ v = clamp_f32{ v = basis, lo = size.min, hi = size.max } }
                }
                else => { return fitted }
            }
        }

        // What the flow children of one element take along an axis: their sizes added up, the
        // largest of them, and the gaps between them.
        struct Extent { sum: f32, largest: f32, gaps: f32 }

        fn measure_children { ui: Ui, first: u32, gap: f32, wide: bool } -> Extent {
            // A gap that is not a number is none.
            let between: f32 = if gap > LIMIT { LIMIT } else if gap < -LIMIT { -LIMIT } else if gap == gap { gap } else { 0.0 }
            let mut out = Extent{ sum = 0.0, largest = 0.0, gaps = 0.0 }
            let mut any = false
            let mut c = first
            while c != tree::NONE {
                let at = @as(usize, c)
                c = ui.tree.elements[at].next
                if not is_flow{ e = ui.tree.elements[at] } { continue }
                let size = if wide { ui.tree.elements[at].w } else { ui.tree.elements[at].h }
                out.sum = out.sum + size
                out.largest = pick_max_f32{ a = out.largest, b = size }
                if any { out.gaps = out.gaps + between }
                any = true
            }
            return out
        }

        // The width a text asks for: its one line's, or for wrapped text that holds newlines the
        // width of its longest paragraph, which is as wide as wrapping can leave it.
        fn measure_natural { mut ui: Ui, e: tree::Element } -> f32 {
            if not e.wrap { return e.content_w }
            let text = get_text{ ui, span = e.text }
            let mut to: usize = 0
            while to < text.len and text[to] != '\n' { to = to + 1 }
            if to >= text.len { return e.content_w }
            let mut widest: f32 = 0.0
            let mut from: usize = 0
            while true {
                let width = text::measure_text{ cache = &ui.cache, list = ui.draw, provider = ui.provider, font = e.font, size = e.text_size, text = text[from..to] }
                widest = pick_max_f32{ a = widest, b = width }
                if to >= text.len { break }
                from = to + 1
                to = from
                while to < text.len and text[to] != '\n' { to = to + 1 }
            }
            return widest
        }

        // Steps 1 and 4, along one axis: widths when `wide`, otherwise heights. Every element
        // gets the size it has before any room is shared.
        fn fit_sizes { mut ui: Ui, wide: bool } {
            let mut i = @as(usize, ui.tree.count)
            while i > 0 {
                i = i - 1
                let e = ui.tree.elements[i]
                if e.kind == tree::Kind::paint { continue }
                let mut content = if wide { e.content_w } else { e.content_h }
                if e.kind == tree::Kind::box {
                    let room = measure_children{ ui, first = e.first_child, gap = e.gap, wide }
                    content = if (e.axis == Axis::row) == wide { room.sum + room.gaps } else { room.largest }
                    if wide { ui.tree.elements[i].content_w = content } else { ui.tree.elements[i].content_h = content }
                } else if e.kind == tree::Kind::text and wide {
                    content = measure_natural{ &ui, e }
                }
                let size = fit_size{ size = get_size{ e, wide }, content = content + sum_padding{ e, wide } }
                if wide { ui.tree.elements[i].w = size } else { ui.tree.elements[i].h = size }
            }
        }

        // Whether a child can take spare room along its parent's axis (`grow`), or give room up.
        fn can_resize { e: tree::Element, wide: bool, grow: bool } -> bool {
            if not is_flow{ e } { return false }
            let size = get_size{ e, wide }
            if size.kind != SizeKind::grow { return false }
            let now = if wide { e.w } else { e.h }
            return if grow { now < size.max } else { now > size.min }
        }

        // Gives `amount` of room to the grow children in the list that starts at `first`, or takes
        // it from them: the same share to each that can still change, held at its `max` or `min`,
        // and what is left over shared again, ROUNDS times at most.
        fn resize_children { mut ui: Ui, first: u32, wide: bool, amount: f32, grow: bool } {
            let mut left = amount
            let mut round: u32 = 0
            while round < ROUNDS and left > SLACK {
                round = round + 1
                let mut able: f32 = 0.0
                let mut c = first
                while c != tree::NONE {
                    let at = @as(usize, c)
                    c = ui.tree.elements[at].next
                    if can_resize{ e = ui.tree.elements[at], wide, grow } { able = able + 1.0 }
                }
                if not (able > 0.0) { return }
                let share = left / able
                c = first
                while c != tree::NONE {
                    let at = @as(usize, c)
                    c = ui.tree.elements[at].next
                    if not can_resize{ e = ui.tree.elements[at], wide, grow } { continue }
                    let size = get_size{ e = ui.tree.elements[at], wide }
                    let now = if wide { ui.tree.elements[at].w } else { ui.tree.elements[at].h }
                    let wanted = if grow { pick_min_f32{ a = now + share, b = size.max } } else { pick_max_f32{ a = now - share, b = size.min } }
                    let next = hold_size{ v = wanted }
                    left = left - abs_f32{ x = next - now }
                    if wide { ui.tree.elements[at].w = next } else { ui.tree.elements[at].h = next }
                }
            }
        }

        // Steps 2 and 4, along one axis: each element, whose own size is final by now, gives its
        // children theirs.
        fn share_sizes { mut ui: Ui, wide: bool } {
            let surface = hold_size{ v = if wide { ui.draw.width } else { ui.draw.height } }
            for i in 0..@as(usize, ui.tree.count) {
                let e = ui.tree.elements[i]
                if e.kind == tree::Kind::paint { continue }
                let mut outer = if wide { e.w } else { e.h }
                if e.root {
                    // A root stands in the surface as a placed child stands in its parent.
                    outer = take_room{ size = get_size{ e, wide }, fitted = outer, basis = surface, filling = true }
                    if wide { ui.tree.elements[i].w = outer } else { ui.tree.elements[i].h = outer }
                }
                if e.first_child == tree::NONE { continue }

                let main = (e.axis == Axis::row) == wide
                let inner = hold_size{ v = outer - sum_padding{ e, wide } }
                let fitted = measure_children{ ui, first = e.first_child, gap = e.gap, wide }
                let room = if main { hold_size{ v = inner - fitted.gaps } } else { inner }

                let mut used = fitted.gaps
                let mut c = e.first_child
                while c != tree::NONE {
                    let at = @as(usize, c)
                    c = ui.tree.elements[at].next
                    if ui.tree.elements[at].kind == tree::Kind::paint { continue }
                    let placed = ui.tree.elements[at].placed
                    let before = if wide { ui.tree.elements[at].w } else { ui.tree.elements[at].h }
                    let size = take_room{ size = get_size{ e = ui.tree.elements[at], wide }, fitted = before, basis = if placed { outer } else { room }, filling = placed or not main }
                    if wide { ui.tree.elements[at].w = size } else { ui.tree.elements[at].h = size }
                    if not placed { used = used + size }
                }
                if main {
                    let spare = inner - used
                    if spare > SLACK {
                        resize_children{ &ui, first = e.first_child, wide, amount = spare, grow = true }
                    } else if spare < -SLACK {
                        resize_children{ &ui, first = e.first_child, wide, amount = -spare, grow = false }
                    }
                }

                // The children's sizes along this axis are final now: what they take is the
                // element's content.
                let taken = measure_children{ ui, first = e.first_child, gap = e.gap, wide }
                let content = if main { taken.sum + taken.gaps } else { taken.largest }
                if wide { ui.tree.elements[i].content_w = content } else { ui.tree.elements[i].content_h = content }
            }
        }

        // Step 3: the lines of every text element, at its width. Wrapped text is broken to its
        // inner width; any other text is one line, cut short for its dots when it is marked
        // `Overflow::ellipsis` and does not fit. A text is then as high as its lines.
        fn lay_text { mut ui: Ui } {
            ui.layout.line_count = 0
            let mut lost: u32 = 0
            for i in 0..@as(usize, ui.tree.count) {
                if ui.tree.elements[i].kind != tree::Kind::text { continue }
                let e = ui.tree.elements[i]
                let at = @as(usize, ui.layout.line_count)
                let inner = hold_size{ v = e.w - sum_padding{ e, wide = true } }
                let mut count: u32 = 0
                let mut dots = false
                if at >= ui.layout.lines.len {
                    lost = add_count{ a = lost, b = 1 }
                } else if e.wrap {
                    let text = get_text{ ui, span = e.text }
                    let wrapped = text::wrap_text{ cache = &ui.cache, list = ui.draw, provider = ui.provider, font = e.font, size = e.text_size, text, width = inner, lines = ui.layout.lines[at..] }
                    count = wrapped.lines
                    if @as(usize, wrapped.rest) < text.len { lost = add_count{ a = lost, b = 1 } }
                } else {
                    let mut line = text::Line{ start = 0, end = e.text.len, width = e.content_w }
                    if e.overflow == Overflow::ellipsis and e.content_w > inner + FITS {
                        let text = get_text{ ui, span = e.text }
                        let fit = text::fit_ellipsis{ cache = &ui.cache, list = ui.draw, provider = ui.provider, font = e.font, size = e.text_size, text, width = inner }
                        line = text::Line{ start = 0, end = fit.len, width = fit.width }
                        dots = fit.dots
                    }
                    ui.layout.lines[at] = line
                    count = 1
                }
                ui.tree.elements[i].first_line = ui.layout.line_count
                ui.tree.elements[i].line_count = count
                ui.tree.elements[i].dots = dots
                // A text that lost its lines keeps the height of one.
                ui.tree.elements[i].content_h = if count > 1 { e.line_height * @as(f32, count) } else { e.line_height }
                ui.layout.line_count = ui.layout.line_count + count
            }
            // Texts left without lines are dropped things: counted once, though a frame may be
            // solved twice.
            while ui.layout.reported < lost {
                report_drop{ &ui, kind = ProblemKind::lines_full }
                ui.layout.reported = ui.layout.reported + 1
            }
        }

        // Step 5. Sizes are final when it runs; it may run again after something moved.
        fn position { mut ui: Ui } {
            let mut i: usize = 0
            while i < @as(usize, ui.tree.count) {
                let mut e = ui.tree.elements[i]
                i = i + 1
                if e.kind == tree::Kind::paint { continue }
                if e.root {
                    e.x = ui.draw.width * e.anchor_x + e.offset_x
                    e.y = ui.draw.height * e.anchor_y + e.offset_y
                    ui.tree.elements[i - 1].x = e.x
                    ui.tree.elements[i - 1].y = e.y
                }
                if e.first_child == tree::NONE { continue }

                let row = e.axis == Axis::row
                let inner_w = e.w - e.padding.left - e.padding.right
                let inner_h = e.h - e.padding.top - e.padding.bottom
                let start_x = e.x + e.padding.left - e.scroll_x
                let start_y = e.y + e.padding.top - e.scroll_y

                let mut used: f32 = 0.0
                let mut flowing: u32 = 0
                let mut c = e.first_child
                while c != tree::NONE {
                    let child = ui.tree.elements[@as(usize, c)]
                    if is_flow{ e = child } {
                        used = used + (if row { child.w } else { child.h })
                        flowing = flowing + 1
                    }
                    c = child.next
                }
                if flowing > 1 { used = used + e.gap * @as(f32, flowing - 1) }
                let spare = (if row { inner_w } else { inner_h }) - used
                let mut cursor = pick_max_f32{ a = 0.0, b = spare } * get_factor{ align = if row { e.align_x } else { e.align_y } }
                let across = get_factor{ align = if row { e.align_y } else { e.align_x } }

                c = e.first_child
                while c != tree::NONE {
                    let at = @as(usize, c)
                    let child = ui.tree.elements[at]
                    c = child.next
                    if child.kind == tree::Kind::paint { continue }
                    if child.placed {
                        ui.tree.elements[at].x = e.x + e.w * child.anchor_x + child.offset_x
                        ui.tree.elements[at].y = e.y + e.h * child.anchor_y + child.offset_y
                    } else if row {
                        ui.tree.elements[at].x = start_x + cursor
                        ui.tree.elements[at].y = start_y + pick_max_f32{ a = 0.0, b = inner_h - child.h } * across
                        cursor = cursor + child.w + e.gap
                    } else {
                        ui.tree.elements[at].y = start_y + cursor
                        ui.tree.elements[at].x = start_x + pick_max_f32{ a = 0.0, b = inner_w - child.w } * across
                        cursor = cursor + child.h + e.gap
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
